    "narrative": "The primary focal point for Ghost Ride the WHIP today is **Corbin Carroll**, who clears the field with a dominant 87.14 efficiency score. By drawing a favorable matchup against Tomoyuki Sugano (4.80 xERA) and capitalizing on a stolen base environment where the opposing pitcher\u2019s delivery cadence favors an aggressive approach, **Corbin Carroll** is our clear anchor. **James Wood** (67.31) and **Mickey Moniak** (65.50) also project as high-floor, high-upside options; **James Wood** benefits significantly from leading off against Elder, while **Mickey Moniak** is positioned to maximize his dynamic platoon edge against Michael Soroka. **Tyler Soderstrom** remains a strong play at 73.03, capitalizing on the 19% SP skill boost provided by the Buehler matchup, helping offset the lack of a park factor advantage.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved **Brice Turang**, who sat on the bubble with a 50.85 score. Despite his elite speed and the 4% efficiency boost gained from the opponent\u2019s slow delivery to the plate\u2014a critical factor for our SB-heavy strategy\u2014he fell just short of the efficiency threshold required to displace **Cole Young** or **Jose Caballero**. The optimizer favored the broader profile of our middle infielders today due to the SP skill delta and park advantages, leaving **Brice Turang** on the bench to preserve our game caps. Meanwhile, we are monitoring \"Lineup Pending\" statuses for **Gabriel Moreno**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Mickey Moniak**, **Corbin Carroll**, and **Henry Bolte**. Specifically, **Henry Bolte** is a risk; while his raw score is passable (51.94), his status at the bottom of the order against Walker Buehler provides the thinnest margin for error of any active starter. Caution is also advised regarding the \"Wind In\" flags impacting **Jose Caballero** and **Seiya Suzuki**, which effectively suppress their power-based upside despite otherwise favorable platoon matchups.",
